    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-8079",
      "knowledge_point_id": "01caf021-de3e-5e2a-89ce-ba289b65c0ff",
      "knowledge_point": "Writing a Complex Number in Exponential Form",
      "problem": "Write the complex number $z=1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}$ in exponential form.",
      "worked_solution": "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}"
