      "problem": "Write the complex number $z=-4-3\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})| \\\\  & ≈\\pi +0.643\\,50… \\\\  & ≈3.785.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=5$ and $\\theta =3.785$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.785\\,\\text{i}}\\,.\n$$",
